ing the match result is not the sole/principal basis.\"\n    },\n    \"passage_anchor\": {\n      \"pdf_page\": 11,\n      \"quote\": \"A no match result does not mean a person is inadmissible.\",\n      \"reason\": \"This exact passage anchors the mechanism limiting the AI's role in the consequential decision.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"strongest_counterevidence\": {\n    \"pdf_page\": 14,\n    \"quote\": \"The direct feed of information from the Automated Targeting System to ACE Truck Manifest allows the CBP Officer to examine the shipment, waive the physical examination, or determine whether enforcement actions are warranted\",\n    \"reason\": \"This suggests automated system outputs materially shape consequential upstream inspection and enforcement actions, which cuts against a broad claim that outputs are not a principal basis.\"\n  },\n  \"overall_reason\": \"The PDF provides exact support for a narrow non-principal-basis claim about facial matching within ACE Truck Manifest: officers can disregard the result, manually query records, inspect documents, and AI results alone may not decide admissibility. But it does not clearly identify the specific inventoried AI use called \\\"Entity Resolution,\\\" and it does not expressly tie the described workflow/version to 2025. It also contains counterevidence showing ACE/ATS outputs help drive referrals, examinations, and possible enforcement actions. So this document offers partial but not conclusive support for the 2025 inventory rebuttal.\"\n}"
